 {"id": "TJ-DF-029", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] systemd-directive-probe RestrictNamespaces judge has no host-baseline negative control — on hosts where unprivileged userns is globally denied (e.g. Ubuntu AppArmor unprivileged_userns), the directive reads ENFORCED regardless of the unit's own setting", "description": "Found 2026-09-25 dogfood running scripts/systemd-directive-probe.py (both scopes) as the deploy doc's Step 4 prescribes. Judge at scripts/systemd-directive-probe.py:252-258 treats ANY nonzero RNS_RC as ENFORCED ('unshare --user --pid denied'). But on a host whose kernel/AppArmor denies unprivileged userns globally, the in-unit probe fails for the host reason, not the directive's — indistinguishable from real enforcement. On THIS host the verdict happens to be correct (host unshare --user --pid rc=0 vs in-unit rc=1), but that is luck, not method: every other directive in the probe is paired with a control (CloseOnExec load-control, AF_UNIX inside the payload), this one is not.", "acceptance_criteria": "Add the missing control: run the same unshare probe once OUTSIDE the transient unit and store the host rc; verdict = ENFORCED only when host rc==0 and unit rc!=0; when host rc!=0 emit UNKNOWN ('host denies unprivileged userns — directive effect not distinguishable'), matching the probe's existing UNKNOWN vocabulary.", "created_at": "2026-09-25T02:34:47-05:00", "created_by": "dogfood satellite 2026-09-25", "detail": "Repro: scripts/systemd-directive-probe.py --json --scope user -> RestrictNamespaces ENFORCED 'unshare --user --pid denied (rc=1)'; host control: unshare --user --pid true -> rc=0 (so correct here). On a global-deny host (the documented fs-isolation-probe failure class, e.g. unprivileged_userns AppArmor) the same judge would print ENFORCED with the directive absent.", "attempts": 0}
