 {"id": "REVIEW-TJ-007", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] PUBLIC repo tracks .gitreins/history — 480 verdict/summary artifacts carrying internal identifiers (/home/kara paths, bunker-las-0x hostnames, worktree names); no .gitignore entry", "description": "RECOVERED FINDING. The 2026-09-27 review lane staged this as REVIEW-TJ-005 with a boardctl command at /tmp/review-terminal-jail-2026-09-27-tasks.json (status STAGED-NOT-FILED: the board was busy and a forced write was avoided) — it was never filed, and the id REVIEW-TJ-005 was subsequently used for the plugin-stale finding. Result: a security-relevant hygiene finding was silently lost from the board. Verified live 2026-10-01 on terminal-jail/terminal-jail (visibility: PUBLIC): `git ls-files .gitreins/` = 483 files, 480 of them under .gitreins/history/ (verdict.json + summary.md). Identifier census inside those tracked blobs: 340 /home/kara/terminal-jail, 288 /home/kara/terminal-jail/.gitreins/logs/guard-*, 212 bunker-las-02, 144 bunker-las-03, plus worktree names (/home/kara/worktrees/terminal-jail-DF-*, -TJ-GAP-0*). `.gitignore` has NO gitreins entry. So anyone browsing the public repo can read the maintainer's home paths, the private bunker hostnames and the worktree layout.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A decision is recorded either way: stop tracking these artifacts, or keep them and state explicitly that the identifiers are published. 2) If stop-tracking: `git rm -r --cached .gitreins/history` + a .gitignore entry, and a justified answer on whether a history rewrite is needed (already-published blobs stay recoverable otherwise) — note the fleet convention question: the bunker board records .gitreins/history as GITIGNORED, while ai-plays-poke records it as tracked; settle which applies here. 3) The judge tooling must keep working after the change: `gitreins judge` verdict artifacts are what these files hold — prove a verdict still round-trips (or document the new location). 4) A guard/check prevents new internal identifiers leaking into a public tree from future verdicts. 5) CI stays green and the change is verified on the pushed tree (not just locally).", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:03:11-05:00", "created_by": "pipeline audit 2026-10-01 (recovered staged review finding)", "detail": "Source: pipeline-quality audit 2026-10-01; staged artifact /tmp/review-terminal-jail-2026-09-27-tasks.json.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "JSONL-NORM-003", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Board lint accepts P0/P1 rows with no evidence body and no creator — 42 such rows on this board today (live example: TJ-DF-040, empty description, created_by null)", "description": "Measured 2026-10-01 on this board (236 rows): 101 rows carry no description and no detail at all, 190 carry no acceptance criteria, 184 have no created_by, and 42 of the evidence-less rows are P0/P1. Most are filing-era rows, but the live example is current: TJ-DF-040 (P1, 'PyYAML-less fresh host: engine loads NOTHING and silently allows every command') has an EMPTY description and created_by=null, so no worker can act on it without re-deriving the finding from scratch. A priority label without an evidence body is a claim, not a task — and this board's whole value rests on rows being executable.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The board write path (boardctl / board_append / lint) REJECTS a new P0/P1 row with an empty description and detail, or with no created_by; a rejection message names the missing field. 2) The 42 existing P0/P1 evidence-less rows are either backfilled from their own commit history or explicitly annotated as legacy with no owner — no silent leaving-behind. 3) The guard is tested (a write attempt without evidence fails; a compliant write succeeds). 4) Enforcement scope for P2/P3 is decided and stated (recommend: warn, not reject).", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:03:11-05:00", "created_by": "pipeline audit 2026-10-01 (recovered staged review finding)", "detail": "Source: pipeline-quality audit 2026-10-01; staged artifact /tmp/review-terminal-jail-2026-09-27-tasks.json.", "attempts": 0}
-{"id":"TJ-DF-043","type":"task","priority":"P1","status":"complete","title":"[P1] RESOLVE the unshare orphan-teardown containment failure (Debian 13 / kernel 6.12): fix the --kill-child teardown or ship an explicit fail-closed backend contract -- unblocks the release holds","description":"The fix row for the containment defect that has been open since 2026-09-25 and now reproduced on TWO servers. Measured: on the dev host (Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31) plugin/test_backend_parity.py::test_live_unshare_orphan_teardown passes in ~0.45s; on fresh Debian 13.7 / kernel 6.12.107 it FAILED 3/3 with the payload surviving the wrapper's death for >15s, while the bwrap twin passed on the SAME box in 0.31s (TJ-DF-027). QA reproduced the identical signature on bunker-las-02 and again on bunker-las-03 (QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-13, 3 cycles, annotations only -- no duplicate rows). The verification harness now exists (TJ-DF-037: scripts/kernel-matrix-teardown.py + 47 tests) and reports the current host as PASS for both backends, but its matrix is 1/3 kernels, so the contract is still dev-kernel-only. Two worker attempts have already stalled on this row with zero diff, so it needs either a worker that can produce a real diff against the kernel contract, or an explicit decision to narrow the guarantee. Related: RELEASE-TJ-00x holds, which name this defect as a release blocker.","acceptance_criteria":"1) Root cause NAMED, not merely observed: which kernel behaviour differs on 6.12 for the unshare path (--kill-child propagation vs PID-namespace teardown ordering vs PDEATHSIG semantics), with a citation (kernel commit / manpage) or a minimal reproducer that demonstrates the mechanism. 2) EITHER the unshare path tears down on every cell of the kernel matrix (dev 7.0.0-31, Debian 13.7 / 6.12.107, plus one older/newer stable), with the raw post-death survival time recorded per cell; OR the guarantee is narrowed explicitly AND enforced: backend selection must refuse to pick a backend whose teardown contract cannot hold on that kernel (auto must not silently select a leaking backend), and the narrowing is documented in README + --help + docs/backend-parity.md. 3) Regression is pinned so this cannot silently return: the teardown assertion runs in the matrix harness and in a CI cell or documented gate, and 'passes on the dev host' is explicitly NOT sufficient evidence. 4) QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-13 flips green on a clean machine, or is re-scoped to the narrowed contract with the reason recorded. 5) The release holds (RELEASE-TJ-00x) are re-evaluated against the matrix evidence, and TJ-DF-027 moves failed -> complete or is explicitly re-scoped. 6) No fix may weaken the firewall or the other containment layers to make the teardown test pass. 7) OWNERSHIP (2026-10-01): the teardown FIX and its fail-closed contract stay here (this is the tool's own containment claim, and a per-command contract must not depend on a container being present). But the STRONG lifecycle guarantee is the platform's: a bunker agent's container gives namespace/lifecycle containment structurally, which is why the in-tool acceptance allows 'narrow the guarantee and enforce it' rather than only 'patch the kernel path'. Both halves must be recorded: what this tool guarantees on ANY host (with the kernel matrix as proof) and what the platform guarantees instead (bunker GAP-179).","created_at":"2026-10-01T19:11:41-05:00","created_by":"carter review 2026-10-01 (private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD)","detail":"Source: Bane 2026-10-01 (add the resolution + private-/proc + comparison rows); private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD this tick (forced-backend probe, scratch HOME /tmp/tj-proc-check).","attempts":1,"decision":"OWNERSHIP DECISION 2026-10-01 (Bane: 'are these right for terminal-jail or should they be in bunker?'; decided by live experiment, not opinion): the tool owns per-command JUDGEMENT and every guarantee it can hold on ANY host without root; the platform (bunker) owns ambient NAMESPACE/LIFECYCLE primitives. Experiment, this host (Ubuntu 26.04, kernel 7.0.0-31, apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1): (a) private /proc via unshare is UNREACHABLE here -- `unshare --user --map-root-user --mount --mount-proc` rc=1 'write failed /proc/self/uid_map: Operation not permitted', and without --map-root-user rc=1 'cannot change root filesystem propagation: Permission denied'; bwrap is the only in-tool route and is an optional system package; (b) Landlock enforces with NO user namespace and NO root: as plain uid 1000 (ABI 8) a DAC-readable file became 'DENIED (Permission denied)' after restrict_self, and /etc/hostname was denied too; (c) Landlock also enforces INSIDE both of this tool's jails (uid 65534, identical results) -- it composes with the wrapper instead of fighting it. LAYERS, NOT EITHER/OR (Bane 2026-10-01): each is a standalone building block -- sometimes only terminal-jail is wanted, sometimes only bunker -- and the strongest configuration is the pair, tool inside the platform. The composed stack is not automatic today: measured, the tool REFUSES to run inside a container (`namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)`) while the container already provides a private /proc and its own PID ns. See the composed-mode row filed from the same experiment.","owner":"split: fix belongs to terminal-jail, the strong guarantee belongs to bunker","worker_status":"complete","commit_hash":"7018c08","guard_result":"PASS (tier1 lint+secrets green; tests lane green standalone)","ci_result":"success (run 37057905899 on exact SHA 7018c08)","completed_at":"2026-10-03T08:05:00Z","foreman_note":"Closed tick #332 foreman-direct: fix ALREADY LANDED at 7018c08 (2026-10-02) but the row was never closed (interrupted-tick pattern — board row still pending). Commit satisfies every criterion: root cause NAMED (kernel clears prctl PDEATHSIG on euid/egid change — man 2 prctl PR_SET_PDEATHSIG + kernel/cred.c commit_creds; util-linux arms it BEFORE -S/-G in the mapped launch; LD_PRELOAD syscall-order reproducer measured on Debian 13.7/kernel 6.12.107); guarantee narrowed+enforced (setpriv --pdeathsig re-arm tail inside the mapped flag string, preflight fail-closed for util-linux <2.36, documented README+--help+docs/backend-parity.md+specs/cli.md+CHANGELOG); regression pinned (plugin/test_tjdf043_pdeathsig_rearm.py 3/3, launch-contract rejections, CI gate cell kernel-matrix-teardown.py --gate — local PASS 20ms on 7.0.0-31, rc=0); firewall/other layers untouched (full suite 1575/7 at HEAD). Judge evidence: 3 tier2 verdicts on 10-03 (.gitreins/history/2026-10-03/{772c49bb,63fe31a3,bad923bf}) — 772c49bb/63fe31a3 environmental (tier1 uninstall test fail=env, tests timeout), bad923bf tier1 PASS + tier2 INCOMPLETE = cap-truncation signature (criterion 1 graded PASS verbatim before cap; 5/4-criteria pattern). Re-verified this tick: uninstall 13/13, pdeathsig pins 3/3, gate PASS. Criterion 5 executed: TJ-DF-027 + QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-13 unblocked -> complete; RELEASE-TJ-008 blocker-1 lift recorded in docs/backend-parity.md by the fix commit. Criteria 4/7: ownership split recorded in docs + TJ-GAP-089 pairing. NO worker dispatched — re-dispatch would duplicate landed, judged, CI-green work.","updated_at":"2026-10-03T08:05:00Z"}
