 {"id": "TJ-GAP-088", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Private /proc is bubblewrap-only and the unshare path leaks the host view: unshare --user shows 1084 /proc entries vs 1079 host with /proc/1 = systemd and the host init cmdline readable -- close it, or move the primitive to the platform", "description": "Premise measured live at HEAD 2026-10-01 (this row exists because the review's earlier 'bare mode' probe was actually the AUTO backend, which picks bwrap on this host -- an auto-backend run is NOT evidence about the unshare path). Forced-backend results, same host, same session: (a) TERMINAL_JAIL_JAIL_BACKEND=unshare (bare) FAILS CLOSED -- 'namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1); command not run -- on unprivileged hosts try --user', because bare mode adds --mount-proc, which needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN inside the namespace; (b) TERMINAL_JAIL_JAIL_BACKEND=unshare --user SUCCEEDS but exposes the host: 1084 numeric /proc entries against 1079 on the host, /proc/1 comm = systemd, /proc/1/cmdline readable, /proc/1/root present; the wrapper deliberately omits --mount-proc under --user (see standalone/terminal-jail: 'it requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN and is incompatible with unprivileged user namespaces'), and Ubuntu's AppArmor userns restriction strips exactly those capabilities; (c) bwrap: 5 entries, /proc/1 = bwrap, private procfs -- the documented property, confirmed. Consequences of the leak: a jailed process can enumerate other processes, read same-uid cmdlines/environ, and see host /proc/net. The documented KNOWN-LIMIT is accurate but only prose -- nothing stops a deployment that assumes containment from silently getting the host view. Bane's steer: if the per-command interface is the wrong place for this primitive, it can be provided by the bunker/platform layer instead (a per-agent rootless container already has its own PID namespace and private procfs).", "acceptance_criteria": "DECIDED 2026-10-01 by experiment, not debate. The mapped-root route is closed on this host class: `unshare --user --map-root-user --mount --mount-proc` rc=1 'write failed /proc/self/uid_map: Operation not permitted'; without --map-root-user rc=1 'cannot change root filesystem propagation: Permission denied'. So private /proc is NOT deliverable per-command on Ubuntu/AppArmor hosts, and bwrap (an optional system package) is the only in-tool route. RESOLUTION: bunker owns the primitive (per-agent PID namespace + private procfs, filed there as GAP-179, cross-referenced to their REVIEW-BUNKER-2 which measured a sibling tenant's argv readable from inside a host-user agent); this tool owns the honest boundary -- keep the bwrap backend, keep failing closed when a caller DEMANDS private /proc and bwrap is absent, and state in the verdict/docs which /proc view the caller actually got. Remaining in-tool work: (1) make the demand enforceable rather than prose (a flag/env that fails closed), (2) state the delivered view in the verdict, (3) add the standing probe (entry count + /proc/1 identity) to the parity battery, (4) correct the docs' method note -- an auto-backend run is NOT unshare evidence. EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE now: re-attempting a mapped-root unshare tier on AppArmor hosts; the experiment closed it.", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:11:41-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD)", "detail": "Source: Bane 2026-10-01 (add the resolution + private-/proc + comparison rows); private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD this tick (forced-backend probe, scratch HOME /tmp/tj-proc-check).", "attempts": 0, "decision": "OWNERSHIP DECISION 2026-10-01 (Bane: 'are these right for terminal-jail or should they be in bunker?'; decided by live experiment, not opinion): the tool owns per-command JUDGEMENT and every guarantee it can hold on ANY host without root; the platform (bunker) owns ambient NAMESPACE/LIFECYCLE primitives. Experiment, this host (Ubuntu 26.04, kernel 7.0.0-31, apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1): (a) private /proc via unshare is UNREACHABLE here -- `unshare --user --map-root-user --mount --mount-proc` rc=1 'write failed /proc/self/uid_map: Operation not permitted', and without --map-root-user rc=1 'cannot change root filesystem propagation: Permission denied'; bwrap is the only in-tool route and is an optional system package; (b) Landlock enforces with NO user namespace and NO root: as plain uid 1000 (ABI 8) a DAC-readable file became 'DENIED (Permission denied)' after restrict_self, and /etc/hostname was denied too; (c) Landlock also enforces INSIDE both of this tool's jails (uid 65534, identical results) -- it composes with the wrapper instead of fighting it. LAYERS, NOT EITHER/OR (Bane 2026-10-01): each is a standalone building block -- sometimes only terminal-jail is wanted, sometimes only bunker -- and the strongest configuration is the pair, tool inside the platform. The composed stack is not automatic today: measured, the tool REFUSES to run inside a container (`namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)`) while the container already provides a private /proc and its own PID ns. See the composed-mode row filed from the same experiment.", "owner": "bunker (platform) -- in-tool part stays as the documented fallback"}
 {"id": "PARITY-TJ-001", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Recurring incumbent-parity sweep: re-check terminal-jail against bubblewrap / firejail / nsjail / Docker+gVisor / Anthropic sandbox-runtime / Codex each release and file the capability deltas", "description": "The 2026-09-27 review compared this project against six incumbents and produced the improvement rows now on the board (TJ-GAP-082 Landlock filesystem tier, TJ-GAP-083 Landlock egress allowlist, TJ-GAP-084 resident bridge, TJ-GAP-086 argument-size ceiling, TJ-GAP-087 installer honesty, TJ-GAP-085 provenance -- landed). This row keeps that loop alive instead of letting the comparison be a one-off: the ecosystem moves (Anthropic's sandbox-runtime, Codex's Landlock/Seatbelt modes, firejail/nsjail/bwrap releases), and this project's whole value proposition is 'the only one of these that JUDGES commands before they run'. Comparable precedent for the fleet: the never-done / E2E recurring rows.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) Each sweep re-derives the incumbent state from PRIMARY sources (their repos/docs/changelogs), not from memory or an aggregator, and records a dated delta table: capability, incumbent, terminal-jail, verdict (ahead / parity / behind / n-a). 2) Every genuine gap becomes a board row with criteria and evidence; every gap that no longer exists is explicitly closed with the reason -- no silent carry-over. 3) Unverifiable sources are named as unverified rather than glossed (the review's own rule). 4) Cadence is stated in the row and honoured (per release cut + quarterly at minimum), and the sweep notes which board rows it consumed or produced. 5) At least one concrete comparison item is chosen from the sweep and, if it is cheap and local, implemented rather than only filed -- the point is continuous improvement, not continuous backlog.", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:11:41-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD)", "detail": "Source: Bane 2026-10-01 (add the resolution + private-/proc + comparison rows); private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD this tick (forced-backend probe, scratch HOME /tmp/tj-proc-check).", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-089", "type": "task", "priority": "P1", "status": "pending", "title": "[P1] Composed mode: terminal-jail inside a bunker agent refuses to run at all today -- teach it to detect the outer containment layer and run as the inner layer of the stack (building blocks, together)", "description": "MEASURED 2026-10-01 (Bane's doctrine: terminal-jail should work INSIDE a bunker for more security; each is a building block and the power is the composition). Experiment: ran this tool inside a container, the closest local stand-in for a bunker agent (`docker run --rm python:3.11-slim`). OUTER (platform/container): uid=0, /proc entries=4, /proc/1=bash, own PID namespace -- i.e. the platform already provides exactly the private /proc and PID-ns containment this tool cannot deliver per command on an AppArmor host. INNER (terminal-jail, installed into the container): the FIREWALL works perfectly -- 'rm -rf /' blocked with the block box, rule_id builtin-rm-rf-root, exit 126 -- but the JAIL refuses to run ANYTHING: even a benign `echo inner-layer-ok` exits with 'namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1); command not run -- on unprivileged hosts try --user', and `--user` fails the same way. Cause: the container grants no CAP_SYS_ADMIN (CapEff 00000000a80425fb excludes bit 21) and its seccomp profile blocks the inner user namespace, so the wrapper cannot create a namespace and -- correctly, per its fail-closed contract -- runs nothing. Net effect: the composed stack, which is the strongest deployment this project has, does not work out of the box. The host-capability probes also timed out (15s) inside the container instead of answering quickly, which makes the failure harder to diagnose than it needs to be.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A composed mode exists and is documented: when the tool detects it is already running inside a containment layer that provides the namespace properties (evidence: existing PID-ns inode differing from the init ns, or an explicit env/marker from the platform), it runs the command with the FIREWALL plus whatever inner layers are still available (seccomp, identity scrub, Landlock) instead of refusing outright. 2) It never over-claims: the verdict/report states per layer what is actually enforced (firewall by the tool, namespace/PID/proc by the platform) so a reader cannot mistake the outer layer's guarantee for the tool's. 3) The failure is diagnosable in one line: when namespace creation is unavailable, the message names the cause (missing CAP_SYS_ADMIN / container seccomp profile) and the options (run with the inner userns permitted, or use composed mode). The capability probes must fail fast rather than timing out. 4) The platform side is specified and cross-linked: what a bunker agent container must permit for the inner layer to add value (bunker GAP-179), and what the agent's environment must expose for detection. 5) A composition test joins the battery with three cells: tool alone (host), platform alone (container), BOTH (tool inside container) -- each recording the measured per-layer properties (PID-ns inode, /proc entry count, /proc/1 identity, seccomp state, verdict provenance). 6) No regression to the existing fail-closed behaviour on a plain host: refusing to run when containment is demanded-but-unavailable stays the default.", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:26:25-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (composition experiment: tool inside a container)", "detail": "Evidence: docker run --rm python:3.11-slim, terminal-jail installed into a scratch HOME inside the container; outer layer /proc entries=4 with /proc/1=bash; inner 'rm -rf /' -> rc=126 rule_id builtin-rm-rf-root; inner `echo` -> rc!=0 'namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)'; CapEff 00000000a80425fb (no CAP_SYS_ADMIN); probes timed out at 15s.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-090", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Onboarding, proven: zero-to-contained in five minutes on a FRESH machine -- executed verbatim by the dogfood/QA lane, not just written down", "description": "PM-GAP-004 closed as 'no user-facing onboarding: docs/ has 10 files but no entry point' -- docs now exist (README, docs/quickstart.md, specs/), so the remaining gap is different: nothing PROVES a stranger's path end-to-end on a clean box. Evidence that this matters: the fresh-install smoke only passed for the first time on dogfood run 12 (f3c2aa7) after 11 runs of friction, and the installer still must be run from the repo root (measured: invoked by absolute path from another cwd it prints 'no local checkout detected' and installs NOTHING -- TJ-GAP-087). A quickstart nobody has re-run on a fresh machine is a hypothesis, not onboarding.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) ONE entry point (a single page, linked from README top) that takes a stranger from nothing to a blocked command and a running jail: install, first verdict, first jailed command, how to read the capability probes, uninstall. 2) The dogfood/QA lane executes it VERBATIM on a fresh bunker agent (no dev-host shortcuts, no hidden steps) and the transcript is attached to this row; a failure re-opens the row. 3) It states plainly what the reader gets on their host (the capability matrix from DOC-9) rather than implying universal containment. 4) Measured time budget stated and met (target: under 5 minutes from clone to first blocked command). 5) Every command in the page is copy-pasteable as written: no placeholders, no '<your-user>' gaps, and the absolute-path installer case works (TJ-GAP-087) or the page does not use it. 6) The page is pinned by a doc test so its commands cannot rot silently.", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:29:13-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (Bane: easy onboarding + docs that speak about BOTH projects)", "attempts": 0, "counterpart": "COUNTERPART (bunker): GAP-180 — new-user onboarding proven on a fresh server (spawn → connect → verify which layer contains you). The two pages must agree on the composed story and share the same per-layer numbers; a change to one must be mirrored in the other."}
 {"id": "DOC-9", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Docs that speak about BOTH projects: the composed stack (terminal-jail inside a bunker agent) with one shared per-layer guarantee matrix, and explicit per-layer attribution", "description": "Bane 2026-10-01: the projects are building blocks -- sometimes only terminal-jail is wanted, sometimes only bunker, and the power is the composition, with the tool running INSIDE a bunker for more security. Today neither project documents the other: the tool's README describes backends and rules but never mentions running inside a bunker agent, and its per-host limits are prose scattered across README / COMPATIBILITY.md / docs/backend-parity.md. The composed configuration is also measurably broken right now (TJ-GAP-089), which makes honest documentation more important, not less: a reader must be able to tell which layer enforces what. SHARED PER-LAYER MATRIX (measured 2026-10-01, host = Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31, apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1; container = `docker run --rm python:3.11-slim`): LAYER 1 platform/container -- own PID namespace (inode differs), private procfs (/proc entries = 4, /proc/1 = bash), lifecycle owned by the platform, uid 0 inside with the container's capability set. LAYER 2 tool on a plain host -- per-command verdict with provenance (block exit 126 + rule_id), seccomp filter (Seccomp: 2, CapEff 0 with --seccomp), identity scrub (HOME=/nonexistent USER=nobody), private /proc ONLY via the bwrap backend (5 entries, /proc/1 = bwrap); the unshare --user path exposes the HOST view (1084 entries, /proc/1 = systemd), and on this host class the mapped-root route to a private procfs is closed (uid_map EPERM). LAYER 3 composed (tool inside the platform) -- MEASURED BROKEN TODAY: the firewall half works inside the container (rm -rf / -> blocked, rule_id builtin-rm-rf-root, rc=126) but the tool's own jail refuses to run any command ('namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)') because the container grants no CAP_SYS_ADMIN and its seccomp profile blocks the inner user namespace. Tracked as terminal-jail TJ-GAP-089 + bunker GAP-179.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A 'Composed deployment' section in this repo that states the three configurations (tool alone, platform alone, BOTH) and what each is for, with a runnable quickstart for the composed case that names the platform's requirement (inner user namespace permitted) and what happens when it is not. 2) The SHARED per-layer matrix (layer | who enforces | measured evidence | what the other layer does NOT give you) appears in BOTH repos with identical numbers -- one source of truth, cross-linked, and each copy names the other file. 3) Per-layer attribution is explicit: the tool must never be documented as providing a property the platform actually provides (the composed case is exactly where that mistake would happen). 4) Numbers are pinned by a check (doc test or probe) so they cannot drift apart between the repos. 5) Cross-links: bunker's counterpart row, TJ-GAP-089 (composed mode), TJ-GAP-088 (private /proc ownership), TJ-GAP-082 (Landlock tier), TJ-GAP-090 (onboarding).", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:29:13-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (Bane: easy onboarding + docs that speak about BOTH projects)", "attempts": 0, "counterpart": "COUNTERPART (bunker): DOC-043 — the composed-stack doc on the platform side, carrying the SAME per-layer guarantee matrix (identical numbers, cross-linked both ways). Related here: TJ-GAP-089 (composed mode), TJ-GAP-088 (private /proc ownership), TJ-GAP-082 (Landlock tier), TJ-GAP-090 (onboarding)."}
